SELECT N,
    CASE 
        WHEN P IS NULL THEN 'Root'
        WHEN N NOT IN (SELECT P FROM BST WHERE P IS NOT NULL) THEN 'Leaf'
        ELSE 'Inner'
    END
FROM BST ORDER BY N;

CASE is like an if-else, but for SQL
There are 2 types of CASE:
Searched CASE:
Syntax:
CASE
  WHEN condition1 THEN result1
  WHEN condition2 THEN result2
  ...
  ELSE default_result
END
Note: if ELSE not provided NULL is returned

Simple CASE:
Syntax:
CASE expression
  WHEN value1 THEN result1
  WHEN value2 THEN result2
  ...
  ELSE default_result
END

e.g:
SELECT
    Name,
    CASE Department
        WHEN 'HR' THEN 'Human Resources'
        WHEN 'IT' THEN 'Technology'
        WHEN 'FIN' THEN 'Finance'
        ELSE 'Other'
    END
FROM Employees;
